$\begin{align}
\dfrac{6^{4}}{6}&=\dfrac{6^{4}}{6^1}
\\\\
&=6^{4-1}
\\\\
&=6^3
\end{align}$

This follows from the general rule $\dfrac{x^m}{x^n}=x^{m-n}$. Note that the powers have the same base.

We can also see this is correct by expanding the powers.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{6^{4}}{6^1}&=\dfrac{\overbrace{\cancel 6\cdot  6\cdot  6\cdot 6}^\text{4 times}}{\underbrace{\cancel 6}_\text{1 time}}
\\\\\\
&=\underbrace{6\cdot 6\cdot 6}_\text{3 times}
\\\\
&=6^3
\end{align}$
